{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the Elohim caused it to come to his hand.</b> For he was liable to death at the hand of Heaven. Here Rashi commented: 'From the wicked a wicked man emerges' (from one who killed unintentionally, who is wicked in that he did not wish to go into exile, there emerged a wicked man who climbs a ladder and falls upon him, etc.). Here Rashi [means] this one who killed unintentionally, etc. — one must say: that one who falls upon his fellow seized a knife and killed him, for if you do not say thus, the death by the sword to which he was liable was not fulfilled upon him — and specifically by way of descent, for if by way of ascent he is not liable, as derived from 'ונפל עליו' (and falls upon him). And one cannot say that he should be liable to two exiles, for the man is already killed." "<b>And I will appoint for you a place.</b> When you have cities to dwell in — here is an allusion to the cities of refuge."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The comment is built on Rashi's reading of the verse per the Talmud (Makkot 10b), where the unintentional killer had previously failed to flee to exile; the supposition that the one who falls seized a knife creates the specific death-by-sword fulfillment. The abbreviation צ״‎ל is resolved as 'צריך לומר' (one must say). The final phrase 'גברא קטילא קטיל' is Aramaic: 'the man is [already] killed.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:22.534697360Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}